course: dictionary
lesson: 4979
title: Words from many lands
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some English words come from other languages. Indaba comes from Zulu and Xhosa, and it is used in South African English. You can often work out a new word's meaning from the words around it, which is called its context.
explain: «They held a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow.» This sentence lists four words from different lands. They are all part of one idea: a meeting to talk things over. The list gives you the clue.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it in. Try "They held a palaver, and a meeting, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow." It still makes sense, so indaba means a meeting to talk things over.
fact: «Indaba» means an important meeting or conference to discuss something.
fact: «Indaba» comes from the Zulu and Xhosa languages and is used in South Africa.
fact: In Scouting, «indaba» means a large meeting of Scout leaders.
fact: Different languages can have different words for the same kind of meeting, such as «palaver», «punchayet» and «pow-wow».
exercise: «They held a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow.» What does indaba mean? | A kind of tent | *A meeting to talk things over | A type of dance | A long walk
exercise: Where is the word «indaba» mostly used? | Canada | Australia | Scotland | *South Africa
exercise: In Scouting, what does «indaba» mean? | A knot for tying ropes | A badge for swimming | A camp fire song | *A large meeting of Scout leaders
test: What do «palaver», «indaba», «punchayet» and «pow-wow» all mean? | A kind of food | A type of boat | *A meeting to talk things over | A very loud noise
test: Which languages does «indaba» come from? | French and German | Latin and Greek | Welsh and Scots | *Zulu and Xhosa
exam: Why is the list «a palaver, and an indaba, and a punchayet, and a pow-wow» useful for working out meaning? | Lists never help with meaning | It shows four colours | It is a shopping list | *It shows four words that all mean a meeting, so the context helps
